R+=[
N('old-cahawba','Old Cahawba','Orrville','al','town','ruins',32.31694,-87.10139,'area','public','walk','','1820–1866',
  'Cahaba, also spelled Cahawba, was Alabama\'s first permanent state capital, from 1820 to 1825. It stood where the Cahaba River meets the Alabama, and it flooded often. It stayed the seat of Dallas County until 1866. In the Civil War a cotton warehouse on the riverbank became Castle Morgan, a prison for Union soldiers from 1863 to 1865; about 3,000 men were held there when the town flooded in February 1865. After the county seat moved to Selma the town emptied, and its buildings were taken down for their materials.',
  'Abandoned streets, cemeteries and ruins of the old capital, kept as Old Cahawba Archaeological Park. St. Luke\'s Episcopal Church of 1854 has been moved back to the site.',
  'A state capital that flooded, held a prison camp, and was carried away brick by brick.',
  'Run by the Alabama Historical Commission, with a visitor center at 9518 Cahaba Road, Orrville. From Selma take AL-22 west for 9 miles, turn left on County Road 9 for 5 miles, then left on County Road 2. An admission fee is charged; check the commission\'s page for current hours.',
  [W('Cahaba, Alabama'),['Alabama Historical Commission: Old Cahawba','https://ahc.alabama.gov/properties/cahawba/cahawba.aspx']]),
N('kennecott-mines','Kennecott mill town','McCarthy','ak','industry','standing',61.48506,-142.88811,'site','public','walk','','1911–1938',
  'Prospectors found copper high above the Kennicott Glacier in the summer of 1900. A 196-mile railroad was built from Cordova to reach it. The first ore went out by train in 1911, and the mines produced more than 4.6 million tons of ore before the last train left on November 10, 1938, leaving the camp a ghost town. It was made a National Historic Landmark in 1986, and the National Park Service has been preserving it since 1998.',
  'A mill town strung along the edge of the glacier. The Park Service counts more than seventy-six buildings in the town and mine camps, the largest being the 14-story timber-frame mill. Ore came down to the mill on three aerial tramways.',
  'A 14-story wooden copper mill at the end of a 60-mile gravel road.',
  'Inside Wrangell-St. Elias National Park and Preserve. The 60-mile McCarthy Road is gravel and is not regularly maintained in winter; the Park Service notes that most rental companies do not allow their cars on it. Park at the end of the road, cross the Kennicott River footbridge on foot, then take a private summer shuttle or walk the 5 miles to Kennecott. Public land in the mill town is mixed with private lots; keep off the private ones.',
  [W('Kennecott, Alaska'),['NPS: Kennecott Mines National Historic Landmark','https://www.nps.gov/places/kennecott-mines.htm'],['NPS: Directions to McCarthy Road and Kennecott','https://www.nps.gov/wrst/planyourvisit/directions-mccarthy-rd-and-kennecott.htm'],['NPS: Kennicott River Bridge','https://www.nps.gov/places/000/kennicott-river-bridge.htm']]),
N('fairbank-az','Fairbank','Fairbank','az','town','standing',31.72306,-110.18833,'site','public','walk','','1881–1970s',
  'Fairbank grew up around a railroad built in 1881 and was the closest rail stop to Tombstone, then a silver boomtown. It was named for Nathaniel K. Fairbank, a Chicago investor who helped pay for the railroad. Its population peaked at 478 in 1890. The last residents left in the 1970s when the buildings were judged unsafe, and the Bureau of Land Management took over the land in 1986.',
  'Several buildings along Railroad Avenue, among them the Mercantile Building, which stayed in business until the early 1970s, and the schoolhouse, restored in 2007 as a small museum. An interpretive trail runs through the townsite.',
  'The railroad stop for Tombstone, emptied in the 1970s.',
  'Fairbank Historic Townsite, Bureau of Land Management, inside the San Pedro Riparian National Conservation Area. The trailhead parking area is open sunrise to sunset. The schoolhouse visitor station, run by the Friends of the San Pedro River, is open Friday to Sunday and on Monday holidays.',
  [W('Fairbank, Arizona'),['BLM: Fairbank Historic Townsite','https://www.blm.gov/visit/fairbank-historic-townsite']]),
N('rush-ghost-town','Rush','Yellville','ar','town','ruins',36.1325,-92.57111,'area','public','walk','','1880s–1960s',
  'Farmers found zinc ore on Rush Creek in the 1880s and a mining town followed. During World War I the Rush mines were the center of the zinc industry in Arkansas; ten companies worked 13 mines here. Homes and stores lasted along the creek until the 1960s. The valley is now part of Buffalo National River, and the district was listed on the National Register in 1987.',
  'A row of empty houses and a store along the road, mine openings and waste piles on the bluffs, and a stone smelter of 1886, the oldest structure in the valley. The short Morning Star loop trail has exhibit panels, and the Mine Level Trail runs about a mile and a half past old mine entrances.',
  'The last zinc town in Arkansas with its buildings still up.',
  'National Park Service land in Buffalo National River. From the Arkansas 14 bridge over the river, go four miles and turn right on Marion County Road 6035. A small gravel lot serves the Morning Star trailhead; a trail map is available at the Buffalo Point information station. Stay out of the mine openings.',
  [W('Rush Historic District'),['NPS: Buffalo National River, places to go','https://www.nps.gov/buff/planyourvisit/places-to-go.htm'],['NPS: Rush Valley trail sheet (PDF)','https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/upload/Rush_508.pdf'],['Arkansas Democrat-Gazette: the town of Rush (2014)','https://www.arkansasonline.com/news/2014/aug/14/once-zinc-clamor-fizzled-town-of-rush-d/'],['Arklahoma Hiker: Morning Star loop','https://arkokhiker.org/north-central-ark/rush-morning-star-interpretive-loop-buffalo-river/']]),
N('sutro-baths','Sutro Baths','San Francisco','ca','amuse','ruins',37.78,-122.51361,'site','public','walk','burned','1896–1966',
  'Adolph Sutro, a wealthy entrepreneur who was mayor of San Francisco from 1895 to 1897, built a huge saltwater bathhouse in a cove below the Cliff House. It opened on March 14, 1896, billed as the largest indoor swimming establishment in the world; at high tide the ocean could fill its pools, 1.7 million gallons, in an hour. Developers bought it in 1964 to put up high-rise apartments and began tearing it down. A fire in June 1966, found to be arson, destroyed what was left, and the apartments were never built.',
  'Concrete pool walls and foundations at the edge of the surf, blocked-off stairs and passages, and a tunnel through the rock.',
  'The world\'s largest indoor pool, burned in the middle of its own demolition.',
  'Part of Golden Gate National Recreation Area since 1973, at Lands End just north of the Cliff House. Trails lead down from the Lands End Lookout. Stay on the trails and back from the cliff edges and the surf.',
  [W('Sutro Baths'),['NPS: Sutro Baths history','https://www.nps.gov/goga/learn/historyculture/sutro-baths.htm'],['NPS: Lands End','https://www.nps.gov/goga/planyourvisit/landsend.htm']]),
N('animas-forks','Animas Forks','Silverton','co','town','standing',37.93111,-107.57139,'site','public','drive','','1873–1915',
  'A mining camp 12 miles northeast of Silverton. At 11,200 feet it was one of the highest in North America. The first log cabin went up in 1873 and the post office opened in 1875. In 1884 a blizzard lasting 23 days buried the town under 25 feet of snow, and people dug tunnels from building to building. The Gold Prince Mill closed in 1910, and the post office closed on November 30, 1915.',
  'Nine standing buildings, stabilized in 1997–98 and restored in 2013–14. Brochures and maps are kept at the parking area beside the townsite.',
  'A town above 11,000 feet where a 23-day blizzard sent everyone into snow tunnels.',
  'Bureau of Land Management land on the Alpine Loop, a 65-mile system of unpaved roads. The BLM says the road from Silverton is passable in summer by two-wheel-drive vehicles; the road beyond, over Engineer Pass to Lake City, may need four-wheel drive.',
  [W('Animas Forks, Colorado'),['BLM: Animas Forks','https://www.blm.gov/visit/animas-forks']]),
]
